From: Nick Schuetz <nschuetz@redhat.com>
Subject: [PATCH] BuiltInPackages: gate Stage 1 system-library swaps for Linux distro packagers

Stage 1 of the Fedora-inclusion roadmap (BUNDLED_LIBRARIES.md) migrates
each `ly_associate_package` entry that has a clean Fedora equivalent
from the upstream packages.o3de.org CDN to the system package. This
patch establishes the convention used by all Stage 1 swaps: each
migrated package is wrapped in an `if (NOT LY_USE_SYSTEM_<X>) ... else()
... endif()` gate. Setting `-DLY_USE_SYSTEM_<X>=ON` (which the o3de-rpm
spec does in %build when the matching `system_<lib>` bcond is enabled)
skips the upstream fetcher and falls through to find_package(<X>
REQUIRED).

The find_package call is satisfied by a per-package companion shim
shipped from sources/Find<X>-system.cmake into cmake/3rdParty/Find<X>.cmake
during %prep. cmake/3rdParty/ is on CMAKE_MODULE_PATH (see
cmake/3rdParty.cmake:435), and is searched before cmake's own internal
modules — so our shim takes precedence and produces a 3rdParty::<X>
target with the shape O3DE consumers expect. (The shims `include()`
cmake's stock find module inline to do the actual library/header
lookup, then construct a real INTERFACE IMPORTED target — not an alias
— wrapping the system library by file path. Aliases-of-aliases and the
runtime walker's per-config IMPORTED_LOCATION_* requirement both ruled
out other patterns; see FindZLIB-system.cmake's comment block for
detail.)

Each gate is independent of the others — packagers can opt one bundle
at a time onto the system version without touching the rest. Default
(both flags absent) preserves upstream behavior 100%.

Currently gated:

  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_EXPAT     → sources/Findexpat-system.cmake (case bridge:
                               bundled FindOpenColorIO calls find_package
                               lowercase, cmake stock is FindEXPAT.cmake)
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_SQLITE    → sources/FindSQLite-system.cmake
                               (mikkelsen pattern; cmake's stock
                               FindSQLite3.cmake creates SQLite::SQLite3
                               which is incompatible with O3DE's runtime
                               walker — same reason as FindZLIB shim)
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_TIFF      → sources/FindTIFF-system.cmake
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_FREETYPE  → sources/FindFreetype-system.cmake
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_LUA       → sources/FindLua-system.cmake
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_MIKKELSEN → sources/Findmikkelsen-system.cmake
                               (no cmake-stock find module — locates
                               system mikktspace directly)
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_PNG       → sources/FindPNG-system.cmake
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_LIBSAMPLERATE → sources/Findlibsamplerate-system.cmake
                               (mikkelsen pattern; engine consumes
                               libsamplerate exclusively in Gems/Microphone/
                               and the Linux PAL is a None stub so runtime
                               risk is essentially zero)
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_LZ4       → sources/Findlz4-system.cmake
                               (no cmake-stock find module — locates
                               system liblz4 directly)
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_OPENEXR   → sources/FindOpenEXR-system.cmake +
                               sources/FindImath-system.cmake
                               (two shims for the single OpenEXR-3.1.3-rev4-linux
                               bundle; the bundled FindOpenColorIO.cmake calls
                               find_package(Imath) independently of OpenEXR,
                               so FindImath must resolve on CMAKE_MODULE_PATH
                               regardless of evaluation order)
  - LY_USE_SYSTEM_ZLIB      → sources/FindZLIB-system.cmake

The else-branch's find_package call is load-bearing: gating out
`ly_associate_package` without replacing it means nothing ever triggers
find_package, the target never gets created, and consumers silently
link nothing. Discovered the hard way on build 10418378 (mikkelsen-only
gate without else-branch — Tier 2 swap-consistency test caught it).

Subsequent build 10420422 caught a second-order issue: aliasing cmake's
stock targets directly (e.g. `add_library(3rdParty::ZLIB ALIAS
ZLIB::ZLIB)`) trips engine-side `add_library(3rdParty::zlib ALIAS
3rdParty::ZLIB)` (alias-of-alias error) and runtime-dependency walking
(per-config IMPORTED_LOCATION_* requirement). The shim approach side-
steps both by creating a real INTERFACE IMPORTED target and linking
the system library via file path rather than via the stock target.

